# Extra-keys row above the phone keyboard

## What to build

A phone keyboard has no Esc, Tab, Ctrl or arrow keys, and the Terminals page's `Ctrl Space` prefix can't be typed on one. Agent CLIs such as Claude Code need these keys all the time.

This slice adds a toolbar `components/ExtraKeys.tsx` to graph-manager's web UI (`apps/graph-manager/src/web`):
- It renders at the bottom of the Terminals page, which is already sized to the space above the phone keyboard, so the row sits just above the keyboard.
- It shows only while `(pointer: coarse)` matches and a terminal on the page has focus.
- Its keys are `Esc`, `Tab`, a sticky `Ctrl`, `↑ ↓ ← →` and the `Ctrl Space` prefix (`⌃␣`).

How the keys reach the terminal:
- The keys send input through a new imperative `TerminalHandle` on `TerminalView`, which calls xterm's `term.input(data, true)`. That goes through `onData`, so predictive echo, `onInput`, `onSubmit` and the WebSocket send see it exactly like typed input.
- Sticky Ctrl lives in the Terminals page. The focused `TerminalView` turns the next single typed character into its control code (`c` → `\x03`). Ctrl clears after one use or when the focused pane changes.
- `⌃␣` calls the page's existing `arm(true)`, so the next key goes to the prefix actions (`n`, `x`, `1`–`4`, `|`).

What the dependencies already delivered (task ids "1" and "5"). They are in the codebase; rely on them:
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/ui/media.ts`:
  - exports `NARROW = "(max-width: 899.98px)"`, `PHONE = "(max-width: 599.98px)"`, `COARSE = "(pointer: coarse)"`;
  - exports `useMedia(query: string): boolean`, which returns false when `window.matchMedia` is missing (jsdom);
  - `pages/Terminals.tsx` already calls `const narrow = useMedia(NARROW)`.
- `ui/viewport.ts`, called from `Shell.tsx`:
  - sets `--app-h` on `<html>` from `visualViewport.height` (`.gm-shell` height is `var(--app-h, 100dvh)`);
  - toggles the class `kb-open` on `<html>` while the keyboard is open.
- `components/touch.ts`:
  - `attachTouch(host, term, { onTap })`: a touch tap calls `term.focus()` synchronously and then `onTap`; a swipe sends synthetic wheel events;
  - `setTextareaAttrs(ta)`: sets autocapitalize/autocorrect/autocomplete off, spellcheck false, enterkeyhint send on xterm's textarea;
  - both are used in `TerminalView.tsx`.
- `TerminalView` has an `onTap?: () => void` prop and a `visualViewport` resize refit. `TerminalPane` passes `onTap={chrome.onFocus}`.
- `Terminals.css`:
  - `touch-action: none` on `.terminal-view` under coarse;
  - `.xterm-helper-textarea` 16 px;
  - `html.kb-open .terms-page.narrow` hides `.terms-topbar` and `.terms-tabbar`;
  - `.terms-page.narrow` hides `.tp-path` and `.tp-max`.
- `TerminalPane.tsx` exports `defaultEcho(saved, coarse)`: instant echo is off by default on touch screens.
- `Terminals.test.tsx`'s `TerminalView` mock records `keyFilters` and `taps` (`onTap`) maps and `data-echo`, and the file has a `stubCoarse(on)` helper that stubs `matchMedia` so only `COARSE` matches. Read the top of that file first. If any of these helpers is missing, add it as described below.

## Context: files to read before implementing

- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/components/TerminalView.tsx`:
  - the props interface (around lines 60-82);
  - the `cb` ref of callbacks (around lines 92-93);
  - `term.onData` (around lines 191-197), where Ctrl and prefix input are rewritten;
  - the `[active]` effect (around lines 222-226).
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/TerminalPane.tsx`:
  - the `TerminalPane` props (around lines 77-100);
  - the `<TerminalView ... />` element (around lines 201-213), to forward the new props.
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/Terminals.tsx`:
  - `PREFIX_MS` (line 45) and `isPrefix`/`MODIFIERS` (lines 86-87);
  - `const [armed, setArmed]` (line 151);
  - `shown` and `focusedItem` (lines 155-156);
  - the prefix machinery (lines 348-399): `armedRef`, `arm()`, `actions.current`, the window keydown capture listener at 377-397, and `keyFilter` at 399;
  - `renderPane` (lines 417-442), where `TerminalPane` gets its props;
  - the markup (lines 448-586): `.terms-main` at 482, `.terms-grid` at 565-567, `SpecFloat` at 570-580, `.prefix-hint` at 582-586.
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/Terminals.css`:
  - `.prefix-hint` (lines 192-198), which sits at `bottom: 20px` of `.terms-page`;
  - the `--term-*` tokens used by the pane chrome (lines 95-160).
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/ui/base.css` lines 213-217: `.toast-stack` (`position: fixed; bottom: var(--space-4)`).
- `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/Terminals.test.tsx`:
  - lines 12-28: the `TerminalView` mock;
  - lines 137-168: `mount`, `prefix()` and `beforeEach`/`afterEach`;
  - lines 300-312: the prefix test pattern: `keyFilters.get("t2")!` returns `false` after `prefix()`.
- xterm 6 typings (`node_modules/@xterm/xterm/typings/xterm.d.ts`; the lockfile pins 6.0.0):
  - `input(data: string, wasUserInput?: boolean): void`;
  - `focus()`;
  - `readonly modes: IModes` with `readonly applicationCursorKeysMode: boolean`.

## Patterns to follow

**Byte sequences (they match what xterm sends for the real keys):**
~~~
Esc   "\x1b"            Tab "\t"
Up    "\x1b[A"  ("\x1bOA" when term.modes.applicationCursorKeysMode)
Down  "\x1b[B"  ("\x1bOB")
Right "\x1b[C"  ("\x1bOC")
Left  "\x1b[D"  ("\x1bOD")
Ctrl+x: a single char matching /[a-z@\[\\\]^_]/i becomes String.fromCharCode(ch.toUpperCase().charCodeAt(0) & 0x1f)
        a→"\x01", c→"\x03", [→"\x1b", @→"\x00"; anything else (e.g. "1", "ab", "\x1b[A") is not changed.
~~~

**Callbacks via a ref, so the xterm effect doesn't re-run (TerminalView.tsx:92-93):**
~~~ts
const cb = useRef({ onExit, onRestart, onConnection, keyFilter, onOutput, onInput, onSubmit, onLatency });
cb.current = { onExit, onRestart, onConnection, keyFilter, onOutput, onInput, onSubmit, onLatency };
~~~
Add `onTap` if it is not already in the list, plus `ctrlArmed`, `onCtrlUsed` and `onPrefixData`.

**State + ref pair for values read inside listeners (Terminals.tsx:351-358, the prefix):**
~~~ts
const armedRef = useRef(false);
const arm = (on: boolean) => {
  armedRef.current = on;
  setArmed(on);
  window.clearTimeout(armTimer.current);
  if (on) armTimer.current = window.setTimeout(() => arm(false), PREFIX_MS);
};
~~~

**Test pattern:** component tests use `@testing-library/react` with `// @vitest-environment jsdom` (see `pages/Terminals.test.tsx`). For `pointerdown`, use `fireEvent.pointerDown(el)` and check the returned boolean: `fireEvent` returns `false` when `preventDefault` was called.

## Step-by-step tasks

### UPDATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/components/TerminalView.tsx`

- **IMPLEMENT**:
  1. Export the handle type and the pure Ctrl helper:
~~~ts
/** What the extra-keys row drives: input as if typed, focus, and the cursor-key mode for arrows. */
export interface TerminalHandle {
  input(data: string): void;
  focus(): void;
  appCursor(): boolean;
}

/** The control code for one character (Ctrl+c → "\x03"), or null when Ctrl doesn't apply to it. */
export function toCtrl(ch: string): string | null {
  if (ch.length !== 1 || !/[a-z@[\\\]^_]/i.test(ch)) return null;
  return String.fromCharCode(ch.toUpperCase().charCodeAt(0) & 0x1f);
}
~~~
  2. New optional props on `TerminalViewProps`, documented like the others:
     - `handleRef?: React.MutableRefObject<TerminalHandle | null>`. Import `type MutableRefObject` from "react".
     - `ctrlArmed?: () => boolean`: sticky Ctrl is on, so the next single character is sent as its control code.
     - `onCtrlUsed?: () => void`: sticky Ctrl was applied.
     - `onPrefixData?: (data: string) => boolean`: sees typed data first; return true to swallow it (the Ctrl Space prefix on Android). See the GOTCHA below.
     Destructure them and add `ctrlArmed`, `onCtrlUsed` and `onPrefixData` to `cb`.
  3. Rewrite the `term.onData` handler:
~~~ts
const input = term.onData((typed) => {
  // A phone keyboard's key after the Ctrl Space prefix arrives here (Android sends keydown "Unidentified").
  if (cb.current.onPrefixData?.(typed)) return;
  let data = typed;
  if (cb.current.ctrlArmed?.()) {
    const c = toCtrl(typed);
    if (c !== null) {
      data = c;
      cb.current.onCtrlUsed?.();
    }
  }
  echo?.input(data);
  send({ type: "input", data });
  cb.current.onInput?.();
  if (data === "\r") cb.current.onSubmit?.();
});
~~~
  4. Expose the handle in a separate effect after the main one. It reads `termRef` lazily, so it survives reconnects:
~~~ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (!handleRef) return;
  const handle: TerminalHandle = {
    input: (data) => termRef.current?.input(data, true),
    focus: () => termRef.current?.focus(),
    appCursor: () => termRef.current?.modes.applicationCursorKeysMode ?? false,
  };
  handleRef.current = handle;
  return () => {
    if (handleRef.current === handle) handleRef.current = null;
  };
}, [handleRef, id]);
~~~
- **GOTCHA**:
  - `term.input(data, true)` fires `onData`. Esc (`"\x1b"`) is one character but doesn't match the Ctrl regex, so it passes through unchanged and Ctrl stays armed. That is intended.
  - Predictive echo (`echo?.input`) must get the rewritten `data`, not `typed`.
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx tsc -p tsconfig.web.json --noEmit`

### CREATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/components/TerminalView.test.ts`

- **IMPLEMENT**: `toCtrl` cases: `a`→`"\x01"`, `A`→`"\x01"`, `c`→`"\x03"`, `z`→`"\x1a"`, `[`→`"\x1b"`, `@`→`"\x00"`, `_`→`"\x1f"`, `1`→`null`, `"ab"`→`null`, `"\x1b[A"`→`null`, `""`→`null`.
- **GOTCHA**: Importing `TerminalView.tsx` pulls in `@xterm/xterm` and its CSS. If the import fails in vitest, add `// @vitest-environment jsdom` at the top. If the CSS import still breaks, move `toCtrl` to `components/ctrl.ts` and re-export it from `TerminalView.tsx`.
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx vitest run src/web/components/TerminalView.test.ts`

### CREATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/components/ExtraKeys.tsx`

- **IMPLEMENT**:
~~~tsx
import type { TerminalHandle } from "./TerminalView";

export type SeqKey = "esc" | "tab" | "up" | "down" | "left" | "right";

/** The bytes a key sends; arrows follow the cursor-key mode as xterm's own keys do (vim, less, agent TUIs). */
export function keySequence(key: SeqKey, appCursor: boolean): string {
  if (key === "esc") return "\x1b";
  if (key === "tab") return "\t";
  const letter = { up: "A", down: "B", right: "C", left: "D" }[key];
  return (appCursor ? "\x1bO" : "\x1b[") + letter;
}

export interface ExtraKeysProps {
  handle: TerminalHandle | null;
  ctrl: boolean;
  onCtrl(on: boolean): void;
  onPrefix(): void;
}
~~~
  Render `<div className="extra-keys" role="toolbar" aria-label="Terminal keys" data-testid="extra-keys">` with eight `<button type="button" className="xk-key">`, in this order:

  | label | aria-label | data-testid | action |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `Esc` | `Esc` | `xk-esc` | `handle?.input(keySequence("esc", ...))` |
  | `Tab` | `Tab` | `xk-tab` | sends `keySequence("tab", ...)` |
  | `Ctrl` | `Ctrl` | `xk-ctrl` | `onCtrl(!ctrl)` |
  | `↑` | `Up` | `xk-up` | sends `keySequence("up", handle?.appCursor() ?? false)` |
  | `↓` | `Down` | `xk-down` | sends `keySequence("down", ...)` |
  | `←` | `Left` | `xk-left` | sends `keySequence("left", ...)` |
  | `→` | `Right` | `xk-right` | sends `keySequence("right", ...)` |
  | `⌃␣` | `Ctrl Space` | `xk-prefix` | `onPrefix()` |

  - The Ctrl button has `aria-pressed={ctrl}`, plus the class `on` while `ctrl` is true.
  - Every button has `onPointerDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()}` and acts on `onClick`. Also add `onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()}`, because iOS fires compat mouse events.
- **GOTCHA**:
  - `preventDefault` on `pointerdown`/`mousedown` keeps focus in xterm's textarea, so the phone keyboard stays open. Do not call `focus()` on the buttons, and don't make the row focusable with `tabIndex` tricks.
  - Arrows read `appCursor()` at click time, not at render time.
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx tsc -p tsconfig.web.json --noEmit`

### CREATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/components/ExtraKeys.test.tsx`

- **IMPLEMENT** (`// @vitest-environment jsdom`, `@testing-library/react`, `cleanup` in `afterEach`):
  - `keySequence` for all six keys in both modes: esc `"\x1b"`, tab `"\t"`, up `"\x1b[A"`/`"\x1bOA"`, down `"\x1b[B"`/`"\x1bOB"`, left `"\x1b[D"`/`"\x1bOD"`, right `"\x1b[C"`/`"\x1bOC"`. Esc and tab are the same in both modes.
  - Render with `handle = { input: vi.fn(), focus: vi.fn(), appCursor: vi.fn(() => false) }`:
    - clicking `xk-esc`, `xk-tab`, `xk-up`, `xk-down`, `xk-left` and `xk-right` calls `handle.input` with the matching sequence;
    - with `appCursor` returning true, `xk-up` sends `"\x1bOA"`.
  - `fireEvent.pointerDown(screen.getByTestId("xk-esc"))` returns `false` (defaultPrevented), and so does every other key.
  - `xk-ctrl` has `aria-pressed="false"` with `ctrl={false}`, and a click calls `onCtrl(true)`. When rerendered with `ctrl={true}`, it has `aria-pressed="true"` and a click calls `onCtrl(false)`.
  - `xk-prefix` shows `⌃␣`, has `aria-label="Ctrl Space"`, and a click calls `onPrefix` once and never calls `handle.input`.
  - The toolbar has `role="toolbar"` and `aria-label="Terminal keys"`.
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx vitest run src/web/components/ExtraKeys.test.tsx`

### UPDATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/TerminalPane.tsx`

- **IMPLEMENT**:
  - Add optional props to `TerminalPane`:
    - `handleRef?: MutableRefObject<TerminalHandle | null>`
    - `ctrlArmed?: () => boolean`
    - `onCtrlUsed?: () => void`
    - `onPrefixData?: (data: string) => boolean`
  - Forward them unchanged to `<TerminalView>`.
- **IMPORTS**: `import TerminalView, { type ConnectionState, type TerminalHandle } from "../components/TerminalView";` and `type MutableRefObject` from "react".
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx tsc -p tsconfig.web.json --noEmit`

### UPDATE `apps/graph-manager/src/web/pages/Terminals.tsx`

- **IMPORTS**: `COARSE` (next to the existing `NARROW`/`useMedia` import from `"../ui/media"`), `ExtraKeys` from `"../components/ExtraKeys"`, and `type TerminalHandle` from `"../components/TerminalView"`.
- **IMPLEMENT**:
  1. `const coarse = useMedia(COARSE);` next to `narrow`.
  2. The focused terminal's handle, behind a stable proxy so `ExtraKeys` never holds a stale one:
~~~ts
const termHandle = useRef<TerminalHandle | null>(null);
const keysHandle = useMemo<TerminalHandle>(() => ({
  input: (d) => termHandle.current?.input(d),
  focus: () => termHandle.current?.focus(),
  appCursor: () => termHandle.current?.appCursor() ?? false,
}), []);
~~~
  3. Sticky Ctrl, as a state + ref pair (the ref is read inside xterm's `onData`):
~~~ts
const [ctrl, setCtrlState] = useState(false);
const ctrlRef = useRef(false);
const setCtrl = useCallback((on: boolean) => {
  ctrlRef.current = on;
  setCtrlState(on);
}, []);
const ctrlArmed = useCallback(() => ctrlRef.current, []);
const onCtrlUsed = useCallback(() => setCtrl(false), [setCtrl]);
useEffect(() => setCtrl(false), [state.focused, focusedItem, setCtrl]); // a pane change drops Ctrl
~~~
  4. Prefix keys typed on a phone keyboard. Define this after `arm`/`actions`:
~~~ts
const onPrefixData = useCallback((data: string) => {
  if (!armedRef.current || data.length !== 1) return false;
  arm(false);
  actions.current(data.toLowerCase());
  return true;
}, []);
~~~
     In the window keydown listener (lines 378-391), add `if (armedRef.current && (e.key === "Unidentified" || e.keyCode === 229)) return;` right after the `isComposing` check. Android's keyboard reports every key that way, and the character then arrives through xterm's `onData` and `onPrefixData`. Keep everything else in the listener as it is.
  5. Track "a terminal on the page has focus":
~~~ts
const [termFocused, setTermFocused] = useState(false);
const keysRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
const inTerminal = (n: EventTarget | null) => n instanceof Element && n.closest(".tp-body") !== null;
const onGridBlur = (e: React.FocusEvent) => {
  const next = e.relatedTarget;
  if (inTerminal(next) || (next instanceof Node && keysRef.current?.contains(next))) return;
  setTermFocused(false);
};
~~~
     On the `.terms-grid` div (line 565), add `onFocus={(e) => setTermFocused(inTerminal(e.target))}` and `onBlur={onGridBlur}`. React's `onFocus`/`onBlur` are `focusin`/`focusout`, so they bubble. `.tp-body` is the wrapper `TerminalPane` puts around `TerminalView`, and xterm's textarea lives inside it.
  6. `const showKeys = coarse && termFocused;`.
     - Render the row inside `<main className="terms-main">`, after the `empty ? … : …` block and before `SpecFloat`. Wrap it in a div so the blur logic can see it:
~~~tsx
{showKeys && (
  <div ref={keysRef} onBlur={onGridBlur}>
    <ExtraKeys handle={keysHandle} ctrl={ctrl} onCtrl={setCtrl} onPrefix={() => arm(true)} />
  </div>
)}
~~~
     - Add the class `xk` to the page section when `showKeys`: `` className={`page terms-page${narrow ? " narrow" : ""}${showKeys ? " xk" : ""}`} ``.
     - Add an effect `useEffect(() => { document.documentElement.classList.toggle("xk-open", showKeys); return () => document.documentElement.classList.remove("xk-open"); }, [showKeys]);`. Toasts are `position: fixed` outside the page.
  7. In `renderPane`, pass to the `TerminalPane` of the focused pane only (`i === state.focused`): `handleRef={termHandle}`, `ctrlArmed={ctrlArmed}`, `onCtrlUsed={onCtrlUsed}` and `onPrefixData={onPrefixData}`. Other panes get `undefined`.
- **GOTCHA**:
  - `arm` is a plain function re-created every render that only touches refs and `setArmed`. Calling it from `onPrefix` and from the empty-deps `useCallback` is safe, because `armedRef`, `armTimer` and `actions` are refs. If eslint's react-hooks plugin warns about missing deps, follow the existing style (the keydown effect at 377-397 also uses `arm` with `[]`).
  - Under 900 px only the focused pane is on screen (`shown = narrow ? [state.focused] : …`, line 155). Keep that.
  - Do not show the row with a fine pointer, even in a narrow window.
- **VALIDATE**: `cd apps/graph-manager && npx tsc -p tsconfig.web.json --noEmit`

